<!--{ we01 : rich }-->
# 1. Welcome

Welcome to Stilo. This tutorial teaches you by doing: each lesson asks you to
try something on its own lines, and many lessons check your work and show
**✓ Done** when it is right. Twelve short lessons, 30 to 45 minutes in all,
take you from your first edit to your own project.

The [OnLabs.org manual](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/) is the reference:
the lessons link to it instead of repeating it.
<!--{ we02 : rich }-->
## What Stilo is

Stilo edits documents that compute, like a mix of Word and Excel. A document
is a stack of lines, as the rows of a spreadsheet, and each line holds text,
a table or a formula. Formulas compute values from the lines above, and from
other documents. A document is a plain text file in a folder, so it can be
copied, sent, compared and kept like any other file.
<!--{ we03 : markdown }-->
## The window

| Part | What it shows |
|------|---------------|
| Top bar | The name of the open document, with a dot (•) while it has unsaved changes. The buttons on the right open the errors, the comments and the commands (⋮). |
| Navigation pane | The documents of the folder, on the left. Click one to open it. Other files (images, PDFs) are greyed out and open in a new tab. |
| The sheet | The open document, as numbered lines. Each line is a *cell*. |
| Right pane | The commands, the comments of a line, or the errors of the document. |

Try it:

- Press **F1**: the commands pane opens on the right, with every command and
  its keys. Press **F1** again to close it.
- Press **Ctrl+B** to hide the navigation pane, and again to show it.
- Press **Alt+T** to switch between the dark and the light theme.

On a Mac, use **⌘** where the lessons say **Ctrl**, and **⌥** (Option) for
**Alt**.
<!--{ we04 : rich }-->
## Your first edit

1. Click the line below that says *My name is Your name*.
2. Press **Enter**: you see the text the line is written with. The part
   between the brackets is a formula; lesson 4 explains formulas.
3. Replace `Your name` with your name. Keep the double quotes around it.
4. Press **Ctrl+Enter** to keep the change. (**Escape** would throw it away.)
5. Press **Ctrl+S** to save. The dot (•) after the document's name in the
   top bar goes away.

The line after yours checks your work: it shows ✓ Done when you have
written your name.
<!--{ we05 : markdown }-->
My name is **<% student: "Your name" %>**.
<!--{ we06 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check ((student != "Your name") & (text.size (text.trim student) > 0)) %>
<!--{ we07 : markdown }-->
## Your copy of the tutorial

These lessons are *your* copy of the tutorial, made by `stilo tutor` in this
folder of your computer:

<% texl.import "~/project.texl" ~> (project -> "`" + project.data.tutorial_folder + "`") %>

Change them as you like: the next time you run `stilo tutor`, they open as
you left them. **Save** your changes (Ctrl+S): the [index](index.texl) counts
the checks of the saved lessons.

To start again from fresh lessons, stop Stilo with **Ctrl+C** in the
terminal where it runs, then run `stilo tutor --reset`. Your old copy is
moved aside, next to the new one, and never deleted.
